  <Callout kind="why">
    Two landing events instead of one exist because two different consumers want two
    different instants. Sound and particle effects want the frame the symbols arrive
    (<code>spin:reelLanding</code>). Game logic that reads the board wants the reel to
    have stopped moving for good (<code>spin:reelLanded</code>). Collapsing them would
    make one of those consumers guess.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Three buses</h2>

  <p>
    Figure 3 shows how the channels relate. Each reel's bus is private to that reel and
    feeds the set bus in exactly one place: the spin controller listens to every
    reel's <code>landing</code> and <code>landed</code> and re-emits them on the set bus
    with the reel index added in front. Nothing else is relayed, so the two buses never
    duplicate each other. The Hold &amp; Win board bus stands apart: it is its own
    emitter with its own vocabulary, never relayed to or from the others. And because
    a <code>BoardGrid</code> cell is a complete one-cell <code>ReelSet</code>, every
    cell on a board also carries the two buses on the left.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={340} caption="The per-reel bus feeds the set bus in exactly one place: SpinController.attachReel() re-emits each reel's landing and landed as spin:reelLanding and spin:reelLanded with the reel index added. The Hold & Win board bus is its own emitter and is never relayed; a BoardGrid cell is a 1x1 ReelSet, so each cell also has the two buses on the left.">
    <Box x={60} y={30} w={300} h={78} title="ReelEvents" kind="accent" sub={['reel.events, one per reel', '7 events, phase:* and landing']} />
    <Box x={60} y={240} w={300} h={78} title="ReelSetEvents" kind="primary" sub={['reelSet.events', '59 events, 13 prefixes']} />
    <Box x={440} y={135} w={240} h={78} title="HoldAndWinBoardEvents" kind="subtle" sub={['board.events, its own bus', '13 events, one object each']} />
    <Edge from={[140, 108]} to={[140, 240]} kind="primary" label="landing → spin:reelLanding" labelDy={-20} />
    <Edge from={[280, 108]} to={[280, 240]} style="dashed" label="landed → spin:reelLanded" labelDy={20} />
  </Diagram>

  <h2>Every ReelSet event</h2>

  <p>
    This is the full reference for the set bus, and the table you will come back to long
    after the rest of the page is familiar. It is grouped by prefix, in roughly the
    order the features appear in a game: the core spin first, then teases and skips,
    then the presentation layers, then the board-reshaping and cascade features that
    most games never touch.
  </p>

  <p>
    How to read it: the <strong>payload</strong> column is the tuple your listener
    receives as its arguments,
    so <code>[reelIndex, symbols]</code> means <code>fn(reelIndex, symbols)</code> and <code>[]</code> means
    the listener takes no arguments. <strong>Fires when</strong> is the exact instant
    and any caveats. <strong>Emitted from</strong> is the file and method
    under <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/</code>, for when you want to read the source
    around it. You do not need the last column to use the event.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class={th}>Event</th>
            <th class={th}>Payload</th>
            <th class={th}>Fires when</th>
            <th class={th}>Emitted from</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {SET_EVENTS.map(([group, rows]) => (
            <>
              <tr class="border-t border-border/60 bg-muted/30">
                <td colspan="4" class="px-4 py-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">{group} · {rows.length}</td>
              </tr>
              {rows.map(([name, payload, when, from]) => (
                <tr class="border-t border-border/60 align-top">
                  <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-primary text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{name}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{payload}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs text-foreground/80">{when}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-muted-foreground">{from}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A few of the groups deserve a sentence of context. <strong>Pins</strong> are claims
    on a cell that force a symbol to stay there across spins (sticky wilds, Hold &amp;
    Win coins). <strong>MultiWays</strong> sets can change how many cells each reel
    shows between spins, which is what the shape and adjust events
    report. <strong>Expand</strong> adds whole reels one step at a time, the
    infinity-reels feature. <strong>Cascade</strong> (also called tumble) is the mode
    where winning symbols are destroyed and new ones fall in from above, and each such
    refill is a new round on the bus. <strong>Nudge</strong> moves one reel by a few
    cells after it has landed. Each has its own page in this tour.
  </p>

  <h2>Per-reel events</h2>

  <p>
    The reel bus is small and lower level. Its job is to narrate one column's progress
    through its phases and to be the original source of the landing
    events. <code>phase:step</code> is the most detailed thing the library emits: every
    phase is made of named steps (the stop phase, for example,
    runs <code>delay</code>, <code>spinOut</code>, <code>land</code> and <code>bounce</code>),
    and this event reports each one starting, ending, or being cut short by a skip. The
    same four columns apply.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class={th}>Event</th>
            <th class={th}>Payload</th>
            <th class={th}>Fires when</th>
            <th class={th}>Emitted from</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {REEL_EVENTS.map(([name, payload, when, from]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60 align-top">
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-primary text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{name}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">{payload}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs text-foreground/80">{when}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-muted-foreground">{from}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    You reach a reel's bus through the set. This is the shape of a listener that only
    cares about one column, for example a sticky wild tracker that needs to know exactly
    when reel 2 came to rest:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`reelSet.getReel(2).events.on('landed', (symbols) => {
  if (symbols.includes('wild')) stickyWilds.push({ reel: 2, ... });
});`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Hold &amp; Win board events</h2>

  <p>
    A Hold &amp; Win board is a grid of cells that each spin on their own, collecting
    coins across a series of respins. It has a different story to tell than a reel set,
    so it has its own bus, <code>HoldAndWinBoard.events</code>, typed
    by <code>HoldAndWinBoardEvents</code>. Every event carries a single object, which
    makes them easy to log and to forward. Two of
    them, <code>respin:start</code> and <code>feature:skip</code>, come from the board
    driver itself; every other one is an effect produced by the pure state machine and
    replayed onto the bus as a <code>{'{ type, payload }'}</code> pair. The state
    machine is explained on
    the <a href="/architecture/pins-and-boards/">pins and boards page</a>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>feature:enter {'{ seed, respins }'}</code>, <code>feature:skip {'{ inFlight, mode, speed?, payload? }'}</code>, <code>feature:reset {'{ clearedCoins }'}</code>, <code>feature:end {'{ coins, rounds, full }'}</code></li>
    <li><code>respin:start {'{ round, respinsLeft, spinning }'}</code>, <code>respin:end {'{ round, hits, respinsLeft }'}</code>, <code>respins:changed {'{ value, reason }'}</code></li>
    <li><code>cell:landed {'{ cell, coin | null }'}</code> in stagger order, <code>coin:locked {'{ coin, locked, capacity }'}</code>, <code>coin:released {'{ coin, remaining }'}</code></li>
    <li><code>board:full {'{ coins }'}</code>, <code>cells:activated {'{ cells, capacity }'}</code>, <code>speed:changed {'{ name, previous }'}</code> (names as strings)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The emitter</h2>

  <p>
    All three buses are instances of one small class, <code>EventEmitter</code>, written
    for this library with no dependencies. It is generic over an event map: a type that
    pairs each event name with a labelled tuple of argument types, which is where the
    autocomplete and the typed payloads come from. Inside it keeps
    a <code>Map</code> from name to listener entries
    (<code>{'{ fn, context, once }'}</code>) and one extra array of wildcard listeners.
    Every method
    except <code>emit</code> and <code>listenerCount</code> returns <code>this</code>,
    so registrations chain.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr><th class={th}>Method</th><th class={th}>Behaviour</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {API.map(([sig, what]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60 align-top">
              <td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-primary text-xs whitespace-nowrap">{sig}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs text-foreground/80">{what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The rules below describe what happens during an <code>emit</code>. Most of them you
    will never notice; the last one you should know about before you ship.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr><th class={th}>Rule</th><th class={th}>What it means for you</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {DISPATCH.map(([rule, what]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60 align-top">
              <td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs font-medium text-foreground">{rule}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs text-foreground/80">{what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A typing caveat, from reading the types rather than from a comment: both event
    maps <code>extend Record&lt;string, unknown[]&gt;</code>, which adds a string index
    signature. A misspelled event name therefore probably still type-checks,
    with <code>unknown[]</code> arguments. Only the declared names get precise argument
    types. ADR 002 says typos are compile errors; the code is the source of truth here,
    so lean on autocomplete rather than on the compiler catching a typo.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    You write <code>reelSet.events.on(...)</code>, not <code>reelSet.on(...)</code>,
    because a <code>ReelSet</code> is a PixiJS container and containers already
    have <code>on</code>, <code>off</code> and <code>emit</code> for pointer events. ADR
    002 keeps the engine's events on their own object so the two vocabularies can never
    collide. The cost is one extra <code>.events.</code> hop.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Watch out</h2>

  <p>
    These are the things that catch people. Each is also stated in the table, but they
    are worth reading together once.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Count <code>spin:reelLanding</code> per stage, not per spin. It fires on the animated stop, on a slam, and again on every cascade refill stage, and nothing deduplicates it.</li>
    <li>Do not wait for a held reel. Reels you hold with <code>spin({'{ holdReels }'})</code> emit no <code>spin:stopping</code> and no <code>spin:reelLanded</code>, yet they count as landed and contribute their unchanged cells to <code>SpinResult.symbols</code>.</li>
    <li>Do not rely on <code>skip:boosted</code> to track the speed. It never fires in cascade mode, nor from <code>slamStop()</code> or <code>requestSkip()</code>, and the restore at the next <code>spin()</code> only emits <code>speed:changed</code>. Listen to <code>speed:changed</code> instead.</li>
    <li>Expect <code>cascade:gravity:*</code> only under <code>mode: 'gravity-then-drop'</code>, and never expect <code>cascade:fall:*</code> on a <code>refill()</code>.</li>
    <li>Handle <code>nudge:cancelled</code> and <code>nudge:complete</code> as alternatives: exactly one of them fires. On cancel the strip still lands deterministically.</li>
    <li>Clean up your own parallel tweens from the <code>signal</code> on every <code>cascade:*:symbol</code> payload. It aborts on a slam, always before the paired <code>:end</code>.</li>
    <li>Only expect <code>pin:migrated</code>, <code>shape:changed</code> and <code>adjust:*</code> on MultiWays sets.</li>
    <li>Prefer <code>off()</code> to a bare <code>removeAllListeners()</code>. With no argument it also removes the <code>onAny</code> hooks, which is what <code>enableDebug().trace()</code> and <code>SpinMetrics</code> rely on.</li>
  </ul>

